WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Kubernetes networking

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:03.780
is famously designed
around core principles that

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.610
can vary in implementation.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.680
Thus, it's essential
to understand

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.660
the specific way
cluster networking works

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.057
when using GKE.

00:00:11.057 --> 00:00:14.536
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.780
There are various resources
we interact with in GKE,

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.170
so let's break this down,
starting with GKE nodes.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.660
In GCP, these nodes
are VMs that live

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.760
within the construct of a VPC--

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.280
in other words, a private
network in the cloud.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.490
Each GKE node gets a
private IP and connectivity

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.580
to the rest of the cluster
and the rest of the network.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.230
At cluster creation
time, you will

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.620
need to define your first
IP range, the node range,

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.110
from which these nodes
will get their IP address.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.450
By default, creating
a GKE cluster

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.230
will also allocate a public
IP to each node as well.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.882
If you want to lock down your
cluster from the internet,

00:00:53.882 --> 00:00:55.340
it's recommended
that you configure

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.600
GKE to run as a private cluster,
in which the nodes will not

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.580
receive a public IP address.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.290
If you want to run
a private cluster

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.600
but also want to establish
outbound connections

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.700
with the internet,
you'll need to configure

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.670
a service like Cloud NAT
for egress to the internet.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.370
The next resource we
want to review in GKE

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:17.060
are pods, the atomic Kubernetes
resource that encompass

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.460
our containerised application.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.970
In Kubernetes, each pod
gets its own IP address.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.420
In GKE, this is accomplished
via alias IP ranges.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.620
This means that each Pod will
have an IP address unique

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.440
in the VPC and can
be natively addressed

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.260
from other clients
in the network.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.660
When you create a
GKE cluster, you

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.260
will need to define your second
IP range, the range from which

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.670
pods will get their IP address.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.170
Each GKE node gets a number
of IPs from this range

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.840
that it can assign to its pods.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.740
By default, a /24 is
carved out for each node,

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.620
which is 256 IPs in total.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.690
Pods can be scheduled
and rescheduled

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:05.820
on various nodes throughout
the lifecycle of the cluster.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.260
One challenge with
this is that it

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.350
means that the IP address
that each pod gets

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.230
may change a number of times.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.420
This brings us to
our final resource

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.120
for which we must configure
an IP range at cluster

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.550
creation, Kubernetes services.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.460
Kubernetes services are how
clients within a cluster

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.830
can access a target set of pods
via a stable virtual IP, known

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.770
as cluster IP in Kubernetes.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.280
At cluster creation time, we
must define a private range

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.100
for those service cluster IPs.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.990
Client pods can
access other pods

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.640
in the cluster via a cluster IP
associated with their service.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.770
This IP address
will remain stable,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.080
even as the target
pods scale up and down

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.025
or shuffle across the cluster.

00:02:51.025 --> 00:02:52.650
Hopefully, you've
gotten a good picture

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.950
of what needs to be configured
at cluster creation time.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.990
Given that this IP
address management

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.950
can require careful planning,
GKE has introduced two features

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:03.640
to make this process easier.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.540
The first feature is
Flexible Pod CIDR.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.510
Earlier, we talked about
how each node, by default,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.680
will get a /24 from which
they can assign IP addresses

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.540
to their Pods.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.810
But what if we only plan on
running 30 pods on a node?

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:23.490
We could potentially
strand many IP addresses.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.870
Flexible Pod CIDR allows users
to reduce the number of IPs

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:33.060
that nodes in a node pool are
given to assign to their pods.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.750
For example, in Node Pool A,
each node is configured to have

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:41.100
a range smaller than a /24
from which they can allocate IP

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.560
addresses to their pods.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.480
This helps users reduce
stranded IP addresses,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.160
which is huge when trying to
plan out cluster networking.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.950
But what do we do when we cannot
predict how many pods we may

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.920
actually need to
run in our cluster?

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.160
This is where the second
GKE feature comes into play,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.550
discontiguous multipod CIDR.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.830
Rather than trying to
get a single pod range

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.050
size correct at
cluster creation time,

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.160
this feature allows users
to incrementally increase

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.310
the IP range provided for pods.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.149
For example, in this diagram, we
have a second pod range in blue

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:22.410
that we have added after
cluster creation for pods.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.990
In summary, we need
to ensure we plan out

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.400
and allocate IP space for
nodes, pods, and services

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.580
when we create a GKE cluster.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.180
GKE provides additional features
to help with this planning,

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.940
enabling users to
preserve IP addresses

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.048
and incrementally grow their
cluster's network over time.

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.590
Now that we've
covered the essentials

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.930
for setting up cluster
networking in GKE,

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.790
visit the links
here to learn more.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.788
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:51.330
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.480
to building a GKE cluster
that suits your needs.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.260
Stay tuned as we expand on other
GKE topics and GKE essentials.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

